(   = \(2 / \(n * pi\)\) * [ -cos\(n*pi\) - \(-cos 0\) ]) Tj
T*
(   = \(2 / \(n * pi\)\) * [ 1 - \(-1\)^n ].) Tj
T*
(   Step 3: Evaluate bn for Even and Odd n:) Tj
T*
(   - If n is even \(n = 2, 4, 6, ...\):) Tj
T*
(   \(-1\)^n = 1 => 1 - 1 = 0 => bn = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - If n is odd \(n = 1, 3, 5, ..., let n = 2k - 1\):) Tj
T*
(   \(-1\)^n = -1 => 1 - \(-1\) = 2 => bn = \(2 / \(n*pi\)\) * 2 = 4 / \(n * pi\).) Tj
T*
(   Step 4: Assemble the Fourier Series:) Tj
T*
(   f\(x\) = sum_{k=1}^infinity [ 4 / \(\(2k - 1\)*pi\) ] * sin\(\(2k - 1\)x\)) Tj
T*
(   = \(4 / pi\) * [ sin x + \(sin 3x\) / 3 + \(sin 5x\) / 5 + \(sin 7x\) / 7 + ... ].) Tj
T*
(   Step 5: Deduction of Leibniz's Series:) Tj
T*
(   - Substitute x = pi / 2 \(a point of continuity where f\(pi/2\) = 1\):) Tj
T*
(   sin\(pi / 2\) = 1) Tj
T*
(   sin\(3*pi / 2\) = -1) Tj
T*
(   sin\(5*pi / 2\) = 1) Tj
T*
(   sin\(7*pi / 2\) = -1.) Tj
T*
(   - Substitute into the Fourier series:) Tj
T*
(   1 = \(4 / pi\) * [ 1 - 1/3 + 1/5 - 1/7 + ... ].) Tj
T*
(   - Multiply both sides by pi / 4:) Tj
T*
(   1 - 1/3 + 1/5 - 1/7 + ... = pi / 4.) Tj
T*
(   Conclusion:) Tj
T*
(   The Fourier series is \(4/pi\)*sum sin\(\(2n-1\)x\)/\(2n-1\), and Leibniz's series pi/4 is verified.) Tj
