(>> Solution to Question 6:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Find the Fourier series of f\(x\) = x on the interval [-pi, pi]. State its value at points of discontinuity and deduce that 1 - 1/3 + 1/5 - 1/7 + ... = pi/4.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Step 1: Symmetry and Coefficients:) Tj
T*
(   f\(-x\) = -x = -f\(x\). Thus f\(x\) = x is an ODD function on [-pi, pi].) Tj
T*
(   a0 = 0 and an = 0 for all n >= 1.) Tj
T*
(   Step 2: Compute Fourier Coefficient bn:) Tj
T*
(   bn = \(2 / pi\) * int_0^pi x * sin\(nx\) dx.) Tj
T*
(   Integrate by parts: u = x, dv = sin\(nx\) dx => du = dx, v = -cos\(nx\)/n.) Tj
T*
(   bn = \(2 / pi\) * [ -x * cos\(nx\) / n ]_0^pi - \(2 / pi\) * int_0^pi [ -cos\(nx\) / n ] dx) Tj
T*
(   = \(2 / pi\) * [ -pi * cos\(n*pi\) / n - 0 ] + \(2 / \(n * pi\)\) * [ sin\(nx\) / n ]_0^pi) Tj
T*
(   = \(2 / pi\) * [ -pi * \(-1\)^n / n ] + 0) Tj
T*
(   = - 2 * \(-1\)^n / n = 2 * \(-1\)^\(n + 1\) / n.) Tj
T*
(   Step 3: Assemble the Fourier Series:) Tj
T*
(   f\(x\) = sum_{n=1}^infinity [ 2 * \(-1\)^\(n+1\) / n ] * sin\(nx\)) Tj
T*
(   = 2 * [ sin x - \(sin 2x\)/2 + \(sin 3x\)/3 - \(sin 4x\)/4 + \(sin 5x\)/5 - ... ].) Tj
T*
(   Step 4: Convergence at Discontinuity:) Tj
T*
(   At endpoints x = -pi and x = pi, the periodic extension has jump discontinuities:) Tj
T*
(   Sum S\(pi\) = \(1/2\) * [ f\(pi-\) + f\(-pi+\) ] = \(1/2\) * [ pi + \(-pi\) ] = 0.) Tj
T*
(   In the Fourier series, every sin\(n*pi\) = 0, so the series converges to 0 at x = +-pi.) Tj
T*
(   Step 5: Deduction of Series:) Tj
T*
(   Substitute x = pi / 2 \(point of continuity where f\(pi/2\) = pi/2\):) Tj
T*
(   pi / 2 = 2 * [ sin\(pi/2\) - \(sin pi\)/2 + \(sin 3pi/2\)/3 - \(sin 2pi\)/4 + \(sin 5pi/2\)/5 - ... ]) Tj
T*
(   pi / 2 = 2 * [ 1 - 0 + \(-1\)/3 - 0 + 1/5 - 0 - 1/7 + ... ]) Tj
T*
(   pi / 2 = 2 * [ 1 - 1/3 + 1/5 - 1/7 + ... ].) Tj
T*
(   Divide both sides by 2:) Tj
T*
(   1 - 1/3 + 1/5 - 1/7 + ... = pi / 4.) Tj
T*
(   Conclusion:) Tj
T*
(   The Fourier series is 2*sum \(-1\)^\(n+1\)*sin\(nx\)/n, and the alternating sum evaluates to pi/4.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
