(   [Problem Statement]: Obtain the Half-Range Cosine Series of f\(x\) = x*\(pi - x\) on the interval [0, pi]. Hence, deduce the sum: sum_{n=1}^infinity 1/n^4 = pi^4 / 90.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Step 1: Compute a0 \(L = pi\):) Tj
T*
(   a0 = \(2 / pi\) * int_0^pi \(pi*x - x^2\) dx = \(2 / pi\) * [ pi*x^2 / 2 - x^3 / 3 ]_0^pi) Tj
T*
(   = \(2 / pi\) * [ pi^3 / 2 - pi^3 / 3 ] = \(2 / pi\) * \(pi^3 / 6\) = pi^2 / 3.) Tj
T*
(   Constant term: a0 / 2 = pi^2 / 6.) Tj
T*
(   Step 2: Compute an:) Tj
T*
(   an = \(2 / pi\) * int_0^pi \(pi*x - x^2\) * cos\(nx\) dx.) Tj
T*
(   Integrate by parts:) Tj
T*
(   u = pi*x - x^2, u' = pi - 2x, u'' = -2, u''' = 0.) Tj
T*
(   v = cos\(nx\), v1 = sin\(nx\)/n, v2 = -cos\(nx\)/n^2, v3 = -sin\(nx\)/n^3.) Tj
T*
(   an = \(2 / pi\) * [ \(pi*x - x^2\)*\(sin nx / n\) - \(pi - 2x\)*\(-cos nx / n^2\) + \(-2\)*\(-sin nx / n^3\)) Tj
T*
(   ]_0^pi.) Tj
T*
(   Sine terms vanish at both limits:) Tj
T*
(   an = \(2 / pi\) * [ \(pi - 2x\) * cos\(nx\) / n^2 ]_0^pi) Tj
T*
(   = \(2 / pi\) * [ \(pi - 2pi\)*cos\(n*pi\) / n^2 - \(pi - 0\)*cos\(0\) / n^2 ]) Tj
T*
ET

