(>> Solution to Question 17:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: State Parseval's Identity for a Fourier series on [-L, L]. Prove it from Euler's formulas and provide its physical interpretation in signal power and energy conservation.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Formal Statement:) Tj
T*
(   Let f\(x\) be square-integrable on [-L, L] with Fourier series:) Tj
T*
(   f\(x\) = a0 / 2 + sum_{n=1}^infinity [ an * cos\(n*pi*x / L\) + bn * sin\(n*pi*x / L\) ].) Tj
T*
(   Then:) Tj
T*
(   \(1 / L\) * int_{-L}^L [f\(x\)]^2 dx = a0^2 / 2 + sum_{n=1}^infinity \(an^2 + bn^2\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Analytical Proof:) Tj
T*
(   - Multiply both sides of the Fourier series by f\(x\) and integrate from -L to L:) Tj
T*
(   int_{-L}^L [f\(x\)]^2 dx = \(a0 / 2\) * int_{-L}^L f\(x\) dx + sum_{n=1}^infinity an * int_{-L}^L) Tj
T*
(   f\(x\)*cos\(n*pi*x / L\) dx + sum_{n=1}^infinity bn * int_{-L}^L f\(x\)*sin\(n*pi*x / L\) dx.) Tj
T*
(   - Recall Euler's formulas:) Tj
T*
(   int_{-L}^L f\(x\) dx = L * a0.) Tj
T*
(   int_{-L}^L f\(x\)*cos\(n*pi*x / L\) dx = L * an.) Tj
T*
(   int_{-L}^L f\(x\)*sin\(n*pi*x / L\) dx = L * bn.) Tj
T*
(   - Substitute these expressions into the right-hand side:) Tj
T*
(   int_{-L}^L [f\(x\)]^2 dx = \(a0 / 2\) * \(L * a0\) + sum_{n=1}^infinity an * \(L * an\) +) Tj
T*
(   sum_{n=1}^infinity bn * \(L * bn\)) Tj
T*
(   = L * [ a0^2 / 2 + sum_{n=1}^infinity \(an^2 + bn^2\) ].) Tj
T*
(   - Divide throughout by L:) Tj
T*
(   \(1 / L\) * int_{-L}^L [f\(x\)]^2 dx = a0^2 / 2 + sum_{n=1}^infinity \(an^2 + bn^2\). \(Proved\).) Tj
T*
(   3. Physical Interpretation:) Tj
T*
(   - In physics and biomedical signal processing, [f\(t\)]^2 represents instantaneous signal power.) Tj
T*
(   - The integral \(1 / 2L\) * int_{-L}^L [f\(t\)]^2 dt represents the Total Average Power \(P_total\)) Tj
T*
(   of the signal.) Tj
T*
(   - Parseval's theorem states:) Tj
T*
(   P_total = P_DC + sum_{n=1}^infinity P_n,) Tj
T*
(   where P_DC = a0^2 / 4 is the DC power component, and P_n = \(an^2 + bn^2\) / 2 is the AC power in) Tj
T*
(   the nth harmonic.) Tj
T*
(   - Thus, total signal power in the time domain EQUALS the sum of powers distributed across all) Tj
T*
(   discrete frequency harmonics \(Rayleigh's Energy Conservation Theorem\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
