(   [Problem Statement]: Apply Parseval's Identity to the Fourier series of f\(x\) = x^2 on [-pi, pi] to evaluate the sum: sum_{n=1}^infinity 1 / n^4.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Step 1: Fourier Series Coefficients of f\(x\) = x^2:) Tj
T*
(   From Subjective 7:) Tj
T*
(   a0 = 2*pi^2 / 3.) Tj
T*
(   an = 4 * \(-1\)^n / n^2.) Tj
T*
(   bn = 0.) Tj
T*
(   Step 2: Parseval's Identity for [-pi, pi]:) Tj
T*
(   \(1 / pi\) * int_{-pi}^pi [f\(x\)]^2 dx = a0^2 / 2 + sum_{n=1}^infinity an^2.) Tj
T*
(   Step 3: Evaluate Left-Hand Side:) Tj
T*
(   [f\(x\)]^2 = \(x^2\)^2 = x^4.) Tj
T*
(   int_{-pi}^pi x^4 dx = 2 * int_0^pi x^4 dx = 2 * [ x^5 / 5 ]_0^pi = 2 * pi^5 / 5.) Tj
T*
(   LHS = \(1 / pi\) * \(2*pi^5 / 5\) = 2*pi^4 / 5.) Tj
T*
(   Step 4: Evaluate a0^2 / 2:) Tj
T*
(   a0^2 = \(2*pi^2 / 3\)^2 = 4*pi^4 / 9.) Tj
T*
(   a0^2 / 2 = 2*pi^4 / 9.) Tj
T*
(   Step 5: Evaluate an^2:) Tj
T*
(   an^2 = [ 4 * \(-1\)^n / n^2 ]^2 = 16 / n^4.) Tj
T*
(   sum_{n=1}^infinity an^2 = 16 * sum_{n=1}^infinity 1 / n^4.) Tj
T*
(   Step 6: Equate and Solve:) Tj
T*
(   2*pi^4 / 5 = 2*pi^4 / 9 + 16 * sum_{n=1}^infinity 1 / n^4) Tj
T*
(   16 * sum_{n=1}^infinity 1 / n^4 = 2*pi^4 / 5 - 2*pi^4 / 9) Tj
T*
(   = 2*pi^4 * \(1/5 - 1/9\) = 2*pi^4 * \(4 / 45\) = 8*pi^4 / 45.) Tj
T*
(   Divide both sides by 16:) Tj
T*
(   sum_{n=1}^infinity 1 / n^4 = \(8*pi^4 / 45\) / 16 = pi^4 / 90.) Tj
T*
(   Conclusion:) Tj
T*
(   Parseval's identity rigorously establishes: sum_{n=1}^infinity 1/n^4 = pi^4 / 90.) Tj
T*
