/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 23:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the Gibbs Phenomenon in Fourier series. Calculate the approximate overshoot near a jump discontinuity of height 2 for a square wave.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Description of Gibbs Phenomenon:) Tj
T*
(   - When a periodic function f\(x\) possessing jump discontinuities is approximated by its) Tj
T*
(   truncated partial Fourier sum S_N\(x\), pronounced oscillations \('ringing'\) appear in the) Tj
T*
(   immediate neighborhood of the discontinuity.) Tj
T*
(   - As N -> infinity \(increasing number of harmonics\), the width of the overshoot spike shrinks) Tj
T*
(   to zero, but its AMPLITUDE does NOT disappear!) Tj
T*
(   - Instead, the peak overshoot approaches a limiting non-zero percentage of the jump height.) Tj
T*
(   2. Mathematical Derivation of Peak Overshoot for Square Wave:) Tj
T*
(   - For f\(x\) = +1 on \(0, pi\) and -1 on \(-pi, 0\), the jump height is:) Tj
T*
(   Jump = f\(0+\) - f\(0-\) = 1 - \(-1\) = 2.) Tj
T*
(   - Truncated partial sum:) Tj
T*
(   S_N\(x\) = \(4 / pi\) * sum_{k=1}^N [ sin\(\(2k - 1\)x\) / \(2k - 1\) ].) Tj
T*
(   - Differentiate S_N\(x\) to find the first local maximum \(overshoot peak\):) Tj
T*
(   S'_N\(x\) = \(4 / pi\) * sum_{k=1}^N cos\(\(2k - 1\)x\) = \(2 / pi\) * [ sin\(2Nx\) / sin\(x\) ].) Tj
T*
(   - Set S'_N\(x\) = 0:) Tj
T*
(   First positive root occurs where sin\(2Nx\) = 0 => 2Nx = pi => x_peak = pi / \(2N\).) Tj
T*
(   - Evaluate S_N\(x_peak\) as a Riemann sum:) Tj
T*
(   S_N\(pi / \(2N\)\) = \(4 / pi\) * sum_{k=1}^N [ sin\(\(2k - 1\)*pi / \(2N\)\) / \(2k - 1\) ]) Tj
T*
(   ~ \(2 / pi\) * int_0^pi [ sin\(t\) / t ] dt = \(2 / pi\) * Si\(pi\).) Tj
T*
(   - The sine integral Si\(pi\) = int_0^pi \(sin t / t\) dt ~ 1.851937.) Tj
T*
(   - S_N\(x_peak\) ~ \(2 / pi\) * 1.851937 = 1.17898.) Tj
T*
(   3. Overshoot Percentage:) Tj
T*
(   - The function steps from 0 to 1 \(step height = 1\).) Tj
T*
(   - Overshoot above the step value 1 is:) Tj
T*
(   Delta_y = 1.17898 - 1.0 = 0.17898.) Tj
T*
(   - Overshoot as a fraction of total jump height 2:) Tj
T*
(   Overshoot = 0.17898 / 2 ~ 0.0895 = 8.95% \(approx 9%\).) Tj
T*
(   Conclusion:) Tj
T*
(   The Gibbs overshoot is an unavoidable property of trigonometric truncation, approaching an) Tj
T*
(   asymptotic overshoot of 8.95% of the total jump.) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
endobj
