(>> Solution to Question 27:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain how Fourier Series decomposition is used in biomedical engineering to analyze Electrocardiogram \(ECG\) signals and filter out 50 Hz powerline interference.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Periodic Structure of ECG Waveforms:) Tj
T*
(   - An ECG records the periodic electrical depolarization and repolarization of heart muscle) Tj
T*
(   tissues \(P-wave, QRS complex, T-wave\).) Tj
T*
(   - For a steady heart rate f0 = 1 / T0 \(typically 1.0 to 1.5 Hz, or 60 to 90 beats/min\):) Tj
T*
(   V_ecg\(t\) = a0 / 2 + sum_{n=1}^infinity [ an * cos\(2*pi*n*f0*t\) + bn * sin\(2*pi*n*f0*t\) ].) Tj
T*
(   2. Frequency Content of ECG Components:) Tj
T*
(   - The fundamental frequency f0 is low \(e.g., 1.2 Hz for 72 bpm\).) Tj
T*
(   - Baseline wander \(respiration, patient motion\) occurs at frequencies below 0.5 Hz \(n = 0, slow) Tj
T*
(   DC drift\).) Tj
T*
(   - P-wave and T-wave contain low frequencies \(0.5 to 10 Hz\).) Tj
T*
(   - QRS complex \(ventricular depolarization\) is a sharp spike containing high harmonic) Tj
T*
(   frequencies: 10 to 40 Hz \(harmonics n ~ 8 to 35\).) Tj
T*
(   - Diagnostic clinical ECG bandwidth is standardized at 0.05 Hz to 150 Hz.) Tj
T*
(   3. Powerline Noise Interference and Fourier Filtering:) Tj
T*
(   - AC mains electromagnetic radiation couples into ECG electrode cables, introducing a strong) Tj
T*
(   sinusoidal contamination at f_noise = 50 Hz \(or 60 Hz in US\).) Tj
T*
(   - In the Fourier domain, this noise manifests as a sharp spectral peak at harmonic index) Tj
T*
(   n_noise = 50 / f0 \(for f0 = 1.2 Hz, n_noise ~ 42\).) Tj
T*
(   - Digital Notch Filter Implementation:) Tj
T*
(   A digital band-stop \(notch\) filter zeroes out the Fourier coefficients in a narrow band around) Tj
T*
(   50 Hz:) Tj
T*
(   an = 0 and bn = 0 for frequencies in [49.5 Hz, 50.5 Hz].) Tj
T*
(   - Reconstructing the signal via inverse Fourier summation produces a clean, noise-free ECG) Tj
T*
(   trace preserving clinical diagnostic morphology.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 28:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: In respiratory biotechnology, lung airflow during breathing is modeled as a periodic square/trapezoidal flow rate. Find the Fourier series for airflow V'\(t\) = V0 on [0, T/2] \(inhalation\) and V'\(t\) = -V0 on [T/2, T] \(exhalation\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Step 1: Set up the Periodic Function:) Tj
T*
(   Period = T. Fundamental angular frequency omega0 = 2*pi / T.) Tj
T*
(   V'\(t\) = +V0 for 0 < t < T/2.) Tj
T*
(   V'\(t\) = -V0 for T/2 < t < T.) Tj
T*
(   Step 2: Shift Variable to Center at Origin:) Tj
T*
(   Let t' = t - T/4.) Tj
T*
(   Then over [-T/2, T/2], V'\(t\) is an even square wave:) Tj
T*
(   V'\(t\) = V0 for -T/4 < t' < T/4, and -V0 elsewhere.) Tj
T*
(   Alternatively, working directly on [0, T]:) Tj
T*
(   Step 3: Compute Mean Value a0:) Tj
T*
(   a0 = \(2 / T\) * int_0^T V'\(t\) dt = \(2 / T\) * [ int_0^\(T/2\) V0 dt + int_{T/2}^T \(-V0\) dt ]) Tj
T*
(   = \(2 / T\) * [ V0 * \(T/2\) - V0 * \(T/2\) ] = 0.) Tj
T*
(   Thus, there is no net accumulation of air volume per complete respiratory cycle \(inhalation =) Tj
T*
(   exhalation\).) Tj
T*
(   Step 4: Compute an:) Tj
T*
(   an = \(2 / T\) * int_0^T V'\(t\) * cos\(n*omega0*t\) dt) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
